{"text": "IAN R. CONNER\n|\nExperience\nKirkland & Ellis LLP\nPartner, Washington, DC October 2011 to Present\nOf Counsel, Washington, DC March 201 | to October 201 1\nAntitrust partner representing merging parties before the U.S. Department of Justice, Federal Trade\nCommission, and state Attorneys General, including handling ten Second Request investigations.\nKey areas of experience include pharmaceuticals, oil and gas, retail services, telecommunications,\ntransportation, and health care. Represent third parties in merger reviews in responding to Civil\nInvestigative Demands and coordinating with federal agencies in litigated merger challenges,\n\nincluding U.S. v. Anthem and FTC v. Advocate Health. Represent class action defendants in multi-\ndistrict antitrust litigation.\n\nHunton & Williams LLP\nAssociate, Competition Team, Washington, DC July 2007 to March 201 1\nDefended BCBS-Michigan in suit brought by Department of Justice alleging MFNs violated antitrust\nlaws. Lead associate representing Delta Air Lines in Department of Justice\u2019s review of merger with\nNorthwest Airlines and related civil lawsuit. Lead antitrust associate in litigation and second-chair at\ntrial in monopolization case against municipal water utility, deposing four apes witnesses and drafting\nresponse to motion to dismiss.\n\nWilliam and Mary School of Law\nAdjunct Professor, \u201cAntitrust & Mergers,\u201d Williamsburg, Virginia Spring 2009 and Spring 2010\n\nU.S. Department of Justice, Antitrust Division, Transportation, Energy & Agriculture Section\nTrial Attorney, Attorney General\u2019s Honors Program, Washington, DC August 2003 to July 2007\nLed investigation of multi-billion dollar merger in the oil and gas industry. Drafted pleadings, Civil\nInvestigative Demands and declarations, and served as liaison to state Attorneys General in U.S. v.\nMonsanto and Delta & Pine Land Company. Drafted Second Request in merger review. Represented\nUnited States on brief in seven asylum and immigration appeals before various U.S. Courts of Appeals.\n\nU.S. Department of Justice, United States Attorney\u2019s Office for the Eastern District of Virginia\nSpecial Assistant United States Attorney, Alexandria, Virginia May 2005 to January 2006\nExamined witnesses, briefed and argued motions, and wrote legal memoranda in multi-week securities\nfraud trial. Represented United States on brief in three criminal appeals before the U.S. Court of\nAppeals for the Fourth Circuit, including U.S. v. Alvarado, 440 F.3d 191 (4th Cir. 2006). First chaired\nseven misdemeanor trials, obtaining guilty verdicts in all seven. Presented cases to the Grand Jury for\nindictment. Negotiated felony plea agreements, and briefed and argued sentencing motions.\n\nAwards: Merger Control Matter of the Year-Americas, Global Competition Review Awards, 2017\nTop 40 Under 40, Global Competition Review, 2015\nOne of five \u201cRising Stars\u201d in Competition by Law360, 2014\n\nAdmissions: Virginia; District of Columbia; U.S. Supreme Court",
